{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/8bmh436l","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":696,"top":394,"width":450,"height":60},"text":"Warning threshold = 3,550.44 MW\nCritical threshold = 5,941.40 MW","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":18,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#D9782D","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/9cfyx876","slideIndex":3,"position":{"left":696,"top":478,"width":450,"height":56},"text":"The thresholds come from the 75th and 90th percentiles of absolute net flow in the training window. This prevents the test period from defining its own labels.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":15,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#263746","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/stvnuj","slideIndex":3,"text":"Source: results/preprocessing_summary.json, results/confusion_matrix.json, and results/model_comparison.json. Explain why the threshold rule is data-based rather than chosen randomly: the training distribution defines the two stress cut points, then the same values label validation and test predictions."}
